// GENERATED by scripts/build-courses.ts from src/lib/courses/content — edit those files, then run: npm run courses
import type { LessonText } from '../types';
const text: LessonText[][] = [[{"say":"AP Chem begins with counting atoms. The mole is the bridge between tiny particles and grams you can weigh.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"One mole contains 6.022 × 10²³ particles. Molar mass (g/mol) converts between grams and moles."},{"t":"key","text":"n = m ÷ M (moles = mass ÷ molar mass). Particles = moles × Avogadro's number."},{"t":"p","text":"A mass spectrum shows the isotopes of an element and their relative abundances. The average atomic mass is the weighted average of the isotope masses."},{"t":"p","text":"Percent composition and empirical formulas: convert mass percents to moles, then divide by the smallest number of moles to get the simplest whole-number ratio."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A compound is 40.0% C, 6.7% H and 53.3% O by mass. Find its empirical formula.","steps":["In 100 g: 40.0/12.01 = 3.33 mol C; 6.7/1.008 = 6.65 mol H; 53.3/16.00 = 3.33 mol O","Divide by 3.33: C 1, H 2, O 1"],"answer":"CH₂O"},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Water of hydration","aim":"Heat a sample of hydrated copper(II) sulfate (CuSO₄·xH₂O) and use the mass lost to find x.","sets":[{"name":"x","start":5,"label":"Waters per formula unit (x)","min":1,"max":10,"step":1},{"name":"m","start":5,"label":"Sample mass before heating (g)","min":1,"max":10,"step":0.5}],"outs":[{"name":"after","expr":"m*159.6/(159.6 + 18.02*x)","label":"Mass after heating (g)"},{"name":"pct","expr":"100*(m - after)/m","label":"Water lost (%)"}],"steps":["Record the mass after heating for several values of x.","A real sample of 5.00 g left 3.20 g. Which x matches?"],"noise":1,"result":"Moles of water ÷ moles of anhydrous salt gives x. A 5.00 g sample leaving 3.20 g matches x = 5 (36% water), so the formula is CuSO₄·5H₂O.","plot":{"x":"x","y":"pct"}}]},{"say":"Where electrons live explains almost every property of the elements. Photoelectron spectroscopy lets us see it directly.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Electron configurations fill orbitals in order of energy: 1s 2s 2p 3s 3p 4s 3d 4p… Aufbau principle, Pauli exclusion (2 electrons per orbital with opposite spins) and Hund's rule (fill orbitals singly first)."},{"t":"key","text":"Coulomb's law drives atomic structure: the attraction between the nucleus and an electron increases with greater nuclear charge and decreases with greater distance."},{"t":"p","text":"Photoelectron spectroscopy (PES) measures the energy needed to remove electrons from each subshell. Each peak is a subshell; its height shows the number of electrons, and its position shows binding energy (core electrons are held more tightly, so they have higher binding energies)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A PES spectrum has peaks with relative heights 2, 2, 6, 1. Which element is it?","steps":["1s² 2s² 2p⁶ 3s¹","Total electrons: 11"],"answer":"Sodium"}]},{"say":"On the AP exam, it's not enough to know the trends. You must explain them with Coulomb's law and shielding.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Atomic radius decreases across a period: more protons (greater effective nuclear charge) pull the same shell closer. It increases down a group: more shells, more shielding.","Ionization energy increases across a period and decreases down a group, for the same reasons.","Electronegativity follows the same pattern as ionization energy.","Cations are smaller than their atoms; anions are larger."]},{"t":"key","text":"Explain trends using effective nuclear charge and distance/shielding, not \"because it's further right.\""},{"t":"p","text":"Successive ionization energies jump sharply when you start removing core electrons. A big jump after the second ionization means the element has 2 valence electrons (group 2)."}]}],[{"say":"Bonds form because atoms are more stable together. Potential energy diagrams show exactly why.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Ionic bonds: transfer of electrons between metals and nonmetals; held by Coulombic attraction in a lattice. Lattice energy increases with larger charges and smaller ions.","Covalent bonds: shared electrons between nonmetals. Polar if electronegativities differ.","Metallic bonds: cations in a sea of delocalized electrons, explaining conductivity and malleability. Alloys can be interstitial (small atoms fill gaps, like steel) or substitutional (similar-size atoms replace each other, like brass)."]},{"t":"key","text":"On a potential energy vs. internuclear distance graph, the minimum energy is at the bond length; the depth of the well is the bond energy."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Potential energy of two H atoms","aim":"Bring two hydrogen atoms together and track the potential energy of the pair.","sets":[{"name":"r","start":120,"label":"Distance between nuclei (pm)","min":50,"max":300,"step":2}],"outs":[{"name":"E","expr":"436*(1 - exp(-0.019*(r - 74)))^2 - 436","label":"Potential energy (kJ/mol)"}],"steps":["Record the energy for at least 6 distances.","Find the distance with the lowest energy."],"noise":0,"result":"Energy is lowest at the bond length (74 pm), where attraction and repulsion balance. The depth of the well (436 kJ/mol) is the bond energy. Closer than this, nuclear repulsion makes energy rise steeply.","plot":{"x":"r","y":"E"}}]},{"say":"Lewis structures predict how atoms connect. When several structures are possible, formal charge picks the best one.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Draw Lewis structures by counting total valence electrons, connecting atoms with single bonds, completing octets on outer atoms, then placing remaining electrons on the central atom (and forming multiple bonds if needed)."},{"t":"key","text":"Formal charge = valence electrons − nonbonding electrons − ½(bonding electrons). The best structure has formal charges closest to zero, with negative charges on more electronegative atoms."},{"t":"p","text":"Resonance occurs when more than one valid structure can be drawn. The real molecule is an average (hybrid), so bonds have intermediate lengths. Ozone and carbonate (CO₃²⁻) are classic examples."},{"t":"p","text":"Exceptions to the octet rule: boron often has 6 electrons; elements in period 3 and beyond (like S and P) can have expanded octets."}]},{"say":"Molecular shape controls polarity, and polarity controls properties. VSEPR predicts shape from electron domains.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Count electron domains (bonds of any type and lone pairs) around the central atom:"},{"t":"list","items":["2: linear (180°), sp","3: trigonal planar (120°), sp²; with one lone pair, bent","4: tetrahedral (109.5°), sp³; one lone pair, trigonal pyramidal; two lone pairs, bent","5: trigonal bipyramidal; 6: octahedral"]},{"t":"key","text":"A molecule is polar if it has polar bonds arranged asymmetrically. CO₂ and CCl₄ are nonpolar because their dipoles cancel."},{"t":"p","text":"Sigma (σ) bonds form from head-on overlap; pi (π) bonds from side-by-side overlap. A single bond is 1 σ; a double bond is 1 σ + 1 π; a triple bond is 1 σ + 2 π."}]}],[{"say":"Intermolecular forces decide boiling points, solubility and more. On the AP exam, always name the specific force.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["London dispersion forces: in all molecules; stronger for larger, more polarizable electron clouds (more electrons).","Dipole–dipole: between polar molecules.","Hydrogen bonding: H bonded to N, O or F interacting with a lone pair on N, O or F. Strongest of the IMFs.","Ion–dipole: between ions and polar molecules (like salt in water)."]},{"t":"key","text":"Stronger IMFs → higher boiling point, higher viscosity, higher surface tension, lower vapor pressure."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Explain why H₂O boils at 100°C but H₂S boils at −60°C.","steps":["Water molecules form hydrogen bonds (H bonded to O)","H₂S has only dipole–dipole and dispersion forces"],"answer":"More energy is needed to overcome water's stronger hydrogen bonds"},{"t":"p","text":"Types of solids: ionic, covalent network (diamond, very hard), molecular (low melting) and metallic."}]},{"say":"Ideal gases follow PV = nRT, but real gases deviate. And solutions have their own math.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"PV = nRT (R = 0.08206 L·atm/mol·K). Real gases deviate most at high pressure and low temperature, when particle volume and attractions matter."},{"t":"list","items":["Kinetic molecular theory: average kinetic energy is proportional to temperature. At the same temperature, lighter gases move faster.","Dalton's law: P_total = sum of partial pressures; partial pressure = mole fraction × total pressure."]},{"t":"p","text":"Solutions: molarity M = moles solute ÷ liters solution. \"Like dissolves like.\" Separation techniques like chromatography and distillation exploit differences in IMFs."},{"t":"p","text":"Spectroscopy: Beer–Lambert law, A = εbc. Absorbance is proportional to concentration, which lets you find an unknown concentration from a calibration curve."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Real gas versus ideal gas","aim":"Compress 1 mol of CO₂ at 300 K and compare its pressure with the ideal gas law.","sets":[{"name":"V","start":1,"label":"Volume (L)","min":0.1,"max":2,"step":0.05},{"name":"T","start":300,"label":"Temperature (K)","min":250,"max":600,"step":10}],"outs":[{"name":"Pi","expr":"0.08206*T/V","label":"Ideal pressure (atm)"},{"name":"Pr","expr":"0.08206*T/(V - 0.04267) - 3.64/V^2","label":"Real (van der Waals) pressure (atm)"},{"name":"dev","expr":"100*(Pr - Pi)/Pi","label":"Difference (%)"}],"steps":["Record the ideal and real pressures at 5 volumes.","Raise the temperature and check whether the gas behaves more ideally."],"noise":1,"result":"At large volumes CO₂ is nearly ideal. When compressed, attractions lower the pressure below ideal; at very small volumes the molecules' own size raises it. Gases are most ideal at high temperature and low pressure.","plot":{"x":"V","y":"Pr"}}]}],[{"say":"AP Chem focuses on what actually reacts. Net ionic equations strip away the spectator ions.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"To write a net ionic equation: write the balanced molecular equation, split strong electrolytes (soluble salts, strong acids and bases) into ions, then cancel ions that appear unchanged on both sides (spectator ions)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Write the net ionic equation for AgNO₃(aq) + NaCl(aq)","steps":["Ions: Ag⁺ + NO₃⁻ + Na⁺ + Cl⁻ → AgCl(s) + Na⁺ + NO₃⁻","Cancel Na⁺ and NO₃⁻"],"answer":"Ag⁺(aq) + Cl⁻(aq) → AgCl(s)"},{"t":"key","text":"All sodium, potassium, ammonium and nitrate salts are soluble. Solids, liquids, gases and weak electrolytes stay together in net ionic equations."},{"t":"p","text":"Stoichiometry uses mole ratios from balanced equations. Find the limiting reactant by calculating how much product each reactant could make."}]},{"say":"Most AP reactions fall into three families: precipitation, acid–base and redox. Let's learn to spot each.","blocks":[{"t":"list","items":["Precipitation: two solutions form an insoluble solid.","Acid–base: proton (H⁺) transfer.","Oxidation–reduction (redox): electron transfer. Oxidation is loss of electrons (oxidation number increases); reduction is gain (oxidation number decreases). \"OIL RIG.\""]},{"t":"key","text":"In a redox reaction, the substance oxidized is the reducing agent, and the substance reduced is the oxidizing agent."},{"t":"p","text":"Titration finds an unknown concentration. At the equivalence point, moles of acid = moles of base (for 1:1 reactions): M_a V_a = M_b V_b."},{"t":"ex","problem":"In Zn + Cu²⁺ → Zn²⁺ + Cu, what is oxidized?","steps":["Zn goes from 0 to +2: loses electrons"],"answer":"Zn is oxidized (and is the reducing agent)"},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Precipitating silver chloride","aim":"Add 0.10 M AgNO₃ to 25.0 mL of 0.10 M NaCl and weigh the dried AgCl precipitate.","sets":[{"name":"V","start":10,"label":"AgNO₃ added (mL)","min":0,"max":50,"step":1}],"outs":[{"name":"m","expr":"min(V*0.1/1000, 0.0025)*143.32","label":"AgCl precipitate (g)"}],"steps":["Record the mass of precipitate for at least 5 volumes.","Find the volume where adding more silver nitrate stops helping."],"noise":2,"result":"Mass increases until 25.0 mL, when moles of Ag⁺ equal moles of Cl⁻; then chloride is limiting and the mass stays at 0.358 g. Gravimetric analysis uses this to find unknown amounts.","plot":{"x":"V","y":"m"}}]}],[{"say":"Kinetics studies how fast reactions go. Rate laws describe exactly how concentration affects speed.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Rate = k[A]ᵐ[B]ⁿ. The orders m and n must be determined experimentally, not from coefficients."},{"t":"p","text":"Use the method of initial rates: compare experiments where one concentration changes and the others stay the same."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Doubling [A] (with [B] constant) quadruples the rate. What is the order in A?","steps":["2ᵐ = 4"],"answer":"Second order in A"},{"t":"p","text":"Integrated rate laws relate concentration to time:"},{"t":"list","items":["Zero order: [A] vs. t is linear.","First order: ln[A] vs. t is linear; half-life t½ = 0.693/k is constant.","Second order: 1/[A] vs. t is linear."]},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Crystal violet fading","aim":"Track the color of crystal violet as it reacts with excess hydroxide, using a spectrometer.","sets":[{"name":"t","start":120,"label":"Time (s)","min":0,"max":600,"step":10},{"name":"OH","start":0.05,"label":"[OH⁻] (mol/L)","min":0.02,"max":0.1,"step":0.01}],"outs":[{"name":"CV","expr":"2.5e-5*exp(-0.2*OH*t)","label":"[crystal violet] (mol/L)"},{"name":"lnCV","expr":"ln(CV)","label":"ln[CV]"}],"steps":["Record ln[CV] every 100 seconds.","Double [OH⁻] and see how the slope changes."],"noise":0.5,"result":"ln[CV] vs time is a straight line, so the reaction is first order in crystal violet; the slope is −k. Doubling [OH⁻] doubles the slope, so it's also first order in hydroxide.","plot":{"x":"t","y":"lnCV"}}]},{"say":"Most reactions happen in a series of simple steps. The slowest step controls the overall rate.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A reaction mechanism is a sequence of elementary steps. For an elementary step, the rate law comes directly from its molecularity (coefficients)."},{"t":"key","text":"The rate-determining step is the slowest step. The overall rate law matches the rate law of that step (substituting for intermediates if needed)."},{"t":"list","items":["Intermediates are produced in one step and consumed in a later step.","Catalysts are consumed in an early step and regenerated later. They provide a new pathway with a lower activation energy and don't appear in the overall equation."]},{"t":"p","text":"Collision theory: reactions need collisions with enough energy (at least Eₐ) and the correct orientation. Raising temperature increases the fraction of particles with enough energy, so k increases."}]}],[{"say":"Thermochemistry tracks heat flowing in and out of reactions. Calorimetry is how we measure it.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Exothermic reactions release heat (ΔH < 0); endothermic reactions absorb heat (ΔH > 0)."},{"t":"key","text":"q = mcΔT. In a calorimeter, heat lost by the reaction = heat gained by the water: q_rxn = −q_water."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A reaction in 100 g of water raises the temperature by 5.0°C. Find q for the reaction. (c = 4.18 J/g·°C)","steps":["q_water = 100 × 4.18 × 5.0 = 2,090 J","The reaction released that heat"],"answer":"q_rxn = −2,090 J (exothermic)"},{"t":"p","text":"Phase changes absorb or release energy at constant temperature: melting and boiling are endothermic; freezing and condensing are exothermic. On a heating curve, flat sections are phase changes."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Heat of neutralization","aim":"Mix 50.0 mL of HCl with 50.0 mL of NaOH of equal concentration in a coffee-cup calorimeter.","sets":[{"name":"c","start":1,"label":"Concentration of each solution (M)","min":0.5,"max":2,"step":0.1}],"outs":[{"name":"n","expr":"c*0.05","label":"Moles of water formed"},{"name":"dT","expr":"57.1*n*1000/(100*4.18)","label":"Temperature rise (°C)"},{"name":"dH","expr":"-100*4.18*dT/n/1000","label":"ΔH (kJ/mol)"}],"steps":["Record the temperature rise for at least 4 concentrations.","Calculate ΔH = −q/n for each and compare."],"noise":3,"result":"The temperature rise is proportional to moles reacted, but ΔH per mole stays about −57 kJ/mol for any strong acid–strong base pair, because the reaction is always H⁺ + OH⁻ → H₂O.","plot":{"x":"c","y":"dT"}}]},{"say":"Enthalpy is a state function, so you can add reactions like puzzle pieces to find ΔH. That's Hess's law.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"Hess's law: if a reaction is the sum of other reactions, its ΔH is the sum of their ΔH values."},{"t":"list","items":["Reversing a reaction changes the sign of ΔH.","Multiplying a reaction by a factor multiplies ΔH by that factor."]},{"t":"p","text":"Using enthalpies of formation: ΔH°rxn = ΣΔH°f(products) − ΣΔH°f(reactants). Elements in their standard states have ΔH°f = 0."},{"t":"p","text":"Using bond enthalpies: ΔH ≈ Σ(bonds broken) − Σ(bonds formed). Breaking bonds requires energy; forming bonds releases energy."},{"t":"ex","problem":"A reaction breaks bonds totaling 1,500 kJ and forms bonds totaling 1,800 kJ. Find ΔH.","steps":["ΔH = broken − formed = 1,500 − 1,800"],"answer":"−300 kJ (exothermic)"}]}],[{"say":"At equilibrium, forward and reverse rates are equal. The equilibrium constant K tells you where the balance lies.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"For aA + bB ⇌ cC + dD, K = [C]ᶜ[D]ᵈ / ([A]ᵃ[B]ᵇ). Pure solids and liquids are left out."},{"t":"list","items":["K ≫ 1: products are favored. K ≪ 1: reactants are favored.","Q (reaction quotient) uses current concentrations. If Q < K, the reaction shifts right; if Q > K, it shifts left; if Q = K, it's at equilibrium."]},{"t":"p","text":"ICE tables (Initial, Change, Equilibrium) organize equilibrium calculations."},{"t":"ex","problem":"For H₂ + I₂ ⇌ 2HI, K = 50. A mixture has Q = 10. Which way will it shift?","steps":["Q < K"],"answer":"It shifts right, making more HI"},{"t":"p","text":"Manipulating K: reversing a reaction gives 1/K; multiplying coefficients by n raises K to the nth power."}]},{"say":"Disturb an equilibrium and it fights back. Le Chatelier's principle predicts how.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A system at equilibrium shifts to counteract any stress: changes in concentration, pressure (volume) or temperature."},{"t":"list","items":["Adding reactant or removing product shifts right.","Decreasing volume (increasing pressure) shifts toward fewer moles of gas.","Temperature changes the value of K: for exothermic reactions, raising temperature shifts left and decreases K.","Catalysts don't shift equilibrium; they just reach it faster."]},{"t":"p","text":"Solubility equilibria: for AgCl(s) ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻, Ksp = [Ag⁺][Cl⁻]. Molar solubility s satisfies Ksp = s² here."},{"t":"p","text":"The common ion effect: adding an ion already in the equilibrium decreases solubility. AgCl is less soluble in NaCl solution than in pure water."},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"The common-ion effect","aim":"Measure how much AgCl dissolves in solutions that already contain chloride ions.","sets":[{"name":"c","start":0.001,"label":"NaCl already present (mol/L)","min":0,"max":0.1,"step":0.001}],"outs":[{"name":"s","expr":"(-c + sqrt(c^2 + 4*1.8e-10))/2","label":"AgCl solubility (mol/L)"}],"steps":["Record the solubility in pure water and at 4 chloride concentrations."],"noise":0,"result":"Adding a common ion shifts the equilibrium AgCl ⇌ Ag⁺ + Cl⁻ to the left (Le Chatelier), so solubility drops from 1.3 × 10⁻⁵ M in pure water to about Ksp/[Cl⁻].","plot":{"x":"c","y":"s"}}]}],[{"say":"Strong acids ionize completely; weak acids barely ionize. Calculating pH for each is a core AP skill.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"pH = −log[H₃O⁺]; pOH = −log[OH⁻]; pH + pOH = 14 at 25°C; Kw = [H₃O⁺][OH⁻] = 1.0 × 10⁻¹⁴."},{"t":"p","text":"For a strong acid, [H₃O⁺] equals the acid concentration. 0.010 M HCl has pH 2."},{"t":"p","text":"For a weak acid HA ⇌ H⁺ + A⁻, Ka = [H⁺][A⁻]/[HA]. If the acid is only slightly ionized, [H⁺] ≈ √(Ka × C)."},{"t":"ex","problem":"Find the pH of 0.10 M acetic acid (Ka = 1.8 × 10⁻⁵).","steps":["[H⁺] ≈ √(1.8 × 10⁻⁵ × 0.10) = √(1.8 × 10⁻⁶) ≈ 1.34 × 10⁻³","pH = −log(1.34 × 10⁻³)"],"answer":"about 2.87"},{"t":"p","text":"Larger Ka (smaller pKa) means a stronger acid. Conjugate pairs: Ka × Kb = Kw."}]},{"say":"Buffers keep pH steady, which is why your blood doesn't swing wildly. Titration curves show how acids and bases react.","blocks":[{"t":"key","text":"A buffer contains a weak acid and its conjugate base. Henderson–Hasselbalch: pH = pKa + log([A⁻]/[HA])."},{"t":"p","text":"When [A⁻] = [HA], pH = pKa. Buffers work best within about ±1 pH unit of the pKa. Buffer capacity is larger when there's more of both components."},{"t":"p","text":"Titration curves:"},{"t":"list","items":["Strong acid + strong base: equivalence point at pH 7.","Weak acid + strong base: equivalence point above 7 (the conjugate base makes it basic). At the half-equivalence point, pH = pKa.","Weak base + strong acid: equivalence point below 7."]},{"t":"ex","problem":"A weak acid titration reaches its half-equivalence point at pH 4.7. What is the acid's pKa?","steps":["At half-equivalence, [HA] = [A⁻]"],"answer":"pKa = 4.7"},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Titrating a weak acid","aim":"Titrate 25.0 mL of 0.10 M weak acid with 0.10 M NaOH and find its pKa from the curve.","sets":[{"name":"Vb","start":12.5,"label":"NaOH added (mL)","min":0,"max":40,"step":0.5},{"name":"pKa","start":4.76,"label":"Acid pKa","min":3,"max":7,"step":0.01}],"outs":[{"name":"_Ka","expr":"10^(-pKa)","label":"Ka"},{"name":"_Vt","expr":"(25 + Vb)/1000","label":"Total volume (L)"},{"name":"_HA","expr":"max(0.0025 - Vb*0.0001, 0)/_Vt","label":"[HA]"},{"name":"_A","expr":"min(Vb*0.0001, 0.0025)/_Vt","label":"[A⁻]"},{"name":"_H1","expr":"(-(_A + _Ka) + sqrt((_A + _Ka)^2 + 4*_Ka*_HA))/2","label":"[H⁺] from the acid"},{"name":"_OH","expr":"max(Vb*0.0001 - 0.0025, 0)/_Vt","label":"Excess [OH⁻]"},{"name":"_OHt","expr":"(_OH + sqrt(_OH^2 + 4*(1e-14/_Ka*_A + 1e-14)))/2","label":"Total [OH⁻]"},{"name":"pH","expr":"-log(max(_H1, 1e-14/_OHt))","label":"pH"}],"steps":["Record the pH at 0, 6, 12.5, 20, 25 and 30 mL.","Compare the pH at the half-equivalence point (12.5 mL) with the pKa."],"noise":0.5,"result":"The curve starts above a strong acid's, has a flat buffer region, and the equivalence point is basic (about 8.7) because A⁻ is a weak base. At half-equivalence [HA] = [A⁻], so pH = pKa.","plot":{"x":"Vb","y":"pH"}}]}],[{"say":"Why do some reactions happen on their own? Entropy and free energy answer that question.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"Entropy (S) measures dispersal of matter and energy. Entropy increases when solids become liquids or gases, when moles of gas increase, and when temperature rises."},{"t":"key","text":"ΔG = ΔH − TΔS. A process is thermodynamically favorable when ΔG < 0."},{"t":"list","items":["ΔH < 0 and ΔS > 0: favorable at all temperatures.","ΔH > 0 and ΔS < 0: never favorable.","ΔH < 0 and ΔS < 0: favorable at low temperatures.","ΔH > 0 and ΔS > 0: favorable at high temperatures."]},{"t":"p","text":"ΔG° relates to K: ΔG° = −RT ln K. If K > 1, ΔG° < 0."},{"t":"oops","text":"\"Thermodynamically favorable\" doesn't mean fast. Diamond turning into graphite is favorable but incredibly slow (kinetic control)."}]},{"say":"Batteries turn chemical energy into electrical energy using redox reactions. Let's see how a galvanic cell works.","blocks":[{"t":"p","text":"A galvanic (voltaic) cell uses a favorable redox reaction to make electricity. Oxidation happens at the anode; reduction at the cathode (\"An Ox, Red Cat\"). Electrons flow through the wire from anode to cathode, and a salt bridge keeps the solutions neutral."},{"t":"key","text":"E°cell = E°cathode − E°anode. A positive E°cell means the reaction is favorable. ΔG° = −nFE°."},{"t":"p","text":"An electrolytic cell uses outside electrical energy to drive an unfavorable reaction, as in electroplating or charging a battery."},{"t":"p","text":"Faraday's law: the amount of substance produced depends on charge: moles of electrons = (current × time) ÷ F, where F = 96,485 C/mol."},{"t":"ex","problem":"E°(Cu²⁺/Cu) = +0.34 V and E°(Zn²⁺/Zn) = −0.76 V. Find E°cell.","steps":["Cu²⁺ is reduced (cathode); Zn is oxidized (anode)","0.34 − (−0.76)"],"answer":"+1.10 V"},{"t":"lab","kind":"lab","title":"Zinc–copper cell and concentration","aim":"Change the ion concentrations in a Zn/Cu galvanic cell and read the voltmeter.","sets":[{"name":"Cu","start":1,"label":"[Cu²⁺] (mol/L)","min":0.001,"max":2,"step":0.001},{"name":"Zn","start":1,"label":"[Zn²⁺] (mol/L)","min":0.001,"max":2,"step":0.001}],"outs":[{"name":"E","expr":"1.10 - 0.0592/2*log(Zn/Cu)","label":"Cell voltage (V)"}],"steps":["Record the voltage at standard conditions (both 1 M).","Then lower [Cu²⁺] and record at least 4 readings."],"noise":0.5,"result":"The Nernst equation E = E° − (0.0592/n) log Q fits the data: lowering the reactant [Cu²⁺] raises Q and lowers the voltage. A cell's voltage drops as it runs and approaches equilibrium (E = 0).","plot":{"x":"Cu","y":"E"}}]}]];
export default text;
